######## ATTADIPA PEDOMETER PROBE ########
Writes CTRL2/CTRL7/CTRL8/CTRL9 and CAL1..CAL4 on the IMU at 0x6B.
Nothing else, on any device. No PMU write, no rail change, no display.

WHO_AM_I  = 0x05   (0x05 in both candidate datasheets)
REVISION  = 0x7C   (0x7C = QMI8658A 13-52-25 Rev A; 0x79 = QMI8658C Rev 0.6)

--- before ---   CTRL2=0x24 CTRL7=0x03 CTRL8=0x00

CTRL7 = 0x00 (aEN=gEN=0, required by 11.2 before configuring) -> ACK
CTRL8 = 0x80 (CTRL9 handshake on STATUSINT, Pedo_EN still 0)  -> ACK
CTRL2 = 0x27 (+/-8 g, 62.5 Hz accel-only)                     -> ACK

two CTRL9 0x0D calls (Table 38): both acknowledged -- CmdDone set and cleared each time
  sample_cnt=62  peak2peak=0x00CC  peak=0x0066  time_up=250
  time_low=25  cnt_entry=10  precision=0  sig_count=4

CTRL8 = 0x90 (Pedo_EN; the 0->1 edge clears the count, 11.6) -> ACK
CTRL7 = 0x01 (aEN; the engine starts counting here, 11.3)     -> ACK
CTRL9 0x0F (reset step count): acknowledged
--- armed  ---   CTRL2=0x27 CTRL7=0x01 CTRL8=0x90  step count = 0

CTRL8 readback: accepted -- the register is writable, which the C Rev 0.6
  document does not describe (it calls CTRL8 Reserved: Not Used)

WHAT TO EXPECT, written down before the walk so it cannot be fitted
afterwards. cnt_entry=10 means the first nine steps are held back and
then counted retroactively; sig_count=4 means the registers move in
fours. Twenty steps at a normal pace should therefore end at 20, having
gone 12, 16, 20 -- and any final value of 16 or 20 is the engine
working. A steady 0 across a real walk is the engine not counting.
STATUS1 bit 4 is the pedometer event (11.4); it should stop being 0x00.

The board may be unplugged from USB now -- a RAM image is not reset by
losing the host, the IMU keeps counting on battery, and this loop picks
up again when the cable comes back. Restore runs at the end either way.

